@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

Tweeta Desktop provides a lightweight desktop interface for Tweetapus.  The
application presents posts, profile data, direct messages, communities, lists,
articles, discovery feeds, account settings, and administrator tools through
native GTK widgets.

The client is intentionally direct: most controls map to one upstream API
operation, and the interface refreshes the affected view after a successful
operation.  Network requests run in background GLib threads where needed so the
GTK main loop remains responsive.

@section What Tweeta Desktop Is For

Tweeta Desktop is useful when you want:

@itemize @bullet
@item A native Linux client for reading public and following timelines.
@item A login-capable desktop client for posting, replying, editing, deleting,
liking, retweeting, quoting, bookmarking, and reacting to posts.
@item Access to Tweetapus-specific features such as communities, lists,
articles, profile labels, delegates, shop/payment workflows, polls, muted
words, and moderation surfaces.
@item A native administrator console for common site operations.
@item GPG-backed encrypted messaging experiments in addition to ordinary
Tweetapus direct messages.
@end itemize

@section Current Scope

The client is broad but intentionally pragmatic.  It implements a large set of
server endpoints, but behavior still depends on the server instance.  Some
features require account privileges, server-side feature flags, a recent
Tweetapus deployment, or a supported Linux desktop environment.

@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start

Install the build dependencies, build the executable, and start it:

@example
make
./tweeta-desktop
@end example

To generate the complete Texinfo documentation set:

@example
make docs
@end example

This produces:

@itemize @bullet
@item @file{tweeta-desktop.info}
@item @file{build/docs/tweeta-desktop.pdf}
@item @file{build/docs/tweeta-desktop.html}
@item @file{build/docs/html/index.html} and related split HTML files
@end itemize

To connect to a different Tweetapus instance for a single launch:

@example
TWEETA_API_BASE_URL="https://example.invalid/api" \
TWEETA_BASE_DOMAIN="https://example.invalid" \
./tweeta-desktop
@end example

Log in with the @samp{Login} button in the header bar or from
@samp{Settings}.  After login, the compose button and authenticated views
become available.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@section Required Dependencies

The Makefile build expects a C compiler and pkg-config metadata for:

@itemize @bullet
@item GTK+ 3.0
@item libcurl
@item json-glib 1.0
@item GPGME
@item GNU Make
@item GNU Texinfo for @command{makeinfo} and, for PDF output,
@command{texi2pdf}
@end itemize

The optional native passkey build uses libfido2.

On Debian or Ubuntu, a typical dependency set is:

@example
sudo apt update
sudo apt install build-essential pkg-config texinfo \
  libgtk-3-dev libcurl4-openssl-dev libjson-glib-dev libgpgme-dev
@end example

For native FIDO2/passkey support, also install:

@example
sudo apt install libfido2-dev
@end example

@section Installing With Make

Build and install under the default prefix, @file{/usr/local}:

@example
make
sudo make install
@end example

The install target installs:

@itemize @bullet
@item @file{$(PREFIX)/bin/tweeta-desktop}
@item @file{$(PREFIX)/share/applications/tweeta-desktop.desktop}
@item @file{$(PREFIX)/share/pixmaps/tweeta-desktop.png}
@item @file{$(PREFIX)/share/man/man1/tweeta-desktop.1}
@item @file{$(PREFIX)/share/info/tweeta-desktop.info}
@end itemize

Set @code{PREFIX} and @code{DESTDIR} for packaging:

@example
make PREFIX=/usr
make DESTDIR="$pkgdir" PREFIX=/usr install
@end example

Uninstall files installed by the Makefile with:

@example
sudo make uninstall
@end example

@section Installing With Meson

Meson builds the application and installs the desktop entry, icon, man page,
and Info manual when @command{makeinfo} is available:

@example
meson setup build
ninja -C build
sudo ninja -C build install
@end example

The Makefile remains the most complete documentation build entry point because
it can generate PDF, Info, single-page HTML, and split HTML from one command.

@node Building
@chapter Building

@section Makefile Build

The default Makefile target builds @file{./tweeta-desktop}:

@example
make
@end example

The Makefile compiles C99 with warnings enabled:

@example
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic-errors -std=c99 -O3 -Isrc
@end example

Library and include flags are discovered with @command{pkg-config}.

@section Optional Native Passkey Build

Build with libfido2 support by setting @code{USE_FIDO2=1}:

@example
make clean
make USE_FIDO2=1
@end example

This adds libfido2 compiler and linker flags and defines @code{USE_FIDO2}.
Without this option, passkey management can still fall back to browser-based
settings where available.

@section Static Build

The Makefile provides a best-effort static target:

@example
make static
@end example

Static GTK builds are frequently not viable because GTK and related libraries
load modules dynamically.  Treat this target as experimental and distribution
dependent.

@section Meson Build

The Meson project builds the same application modules and the GLib test runner:

@example
meson setup build
ninja -C build
ninja -C build test
@end example

@node Invoking tweeta-desktop
@chapter Invoking tweeta-desktop

Run the program as:

@example
tweeta-desktop
@end example

or, from a source checkout after @command{make}:

@example
./tweeta-desktop
@end example

Tweeta Desktop does not define custom command-line options.  Standard GTK3
options are accepted by GTK.

@section Environment Variables

@table @env
@item TWEETA_API_BASE_URL
Overrides the API base URL.  The default is
@url{https://tweeta.tiago.zip/api}.

@item TWEETA_BASE_DOMAIN
Overrides the web/media base domain.  The default is
@url{https://tweeta.tiago.zip}.

@item XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Controls where GLib stores per-user configuration.  Tweeta Desktop stores
session data under @file{tweeta-desktop/session.json} inside this directory.

@item XDG_CACHE_HOME
Controls where GLib stores per-user cache data.  Tweeta Desktop uses a
@file{tweeta-desktop} subdirectory for cache-related operations.

@item G_MESSAGES_DEBUG
Enables GLib debug output when set appropriately, for example
@code{G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all}.

@item G_DEBUG
Can be used with GLib debugging modes such as @code{fatal-criticals} while
developing or diagnosing crashes.
@end table

@node User Interface
@chapter User Interface

@section Main Window

The main window is a GTK window with a header bar and a stacked content area.
The stack contains the timeline, profile, search, notifications, messages,
conversation detail, settings, admin, bookmarks, lists, list detail, explore,
articles, communities, and community timeline views.

@section Header Controls

The header bar includes:

@table @samp
@item Search entry
Enter a query and press @key{Enter} to search users and posts.

@item Back
Returns from secondary views to the previous or main view.

@item Compose
Opens the post composer.  It is enabled after login.

@item Alerts
Opens notifications.

@item Messages
Opens direct messages and encrypted messaging.

@item Bookmarks
Opens bookmarked posts.

@item Lists
Opens owned and followed lists.

@item Explore
Opens discovery categories such as trends, best of week, top hashtags, and
leaderboards.

@item Articles
Opens the article list and article composer.

@item Public/Following
Toggles the main timeline between the public and following feeds.

@item Settings
Opens account, appearance, notification, filter, delegate, shop, and data
settings.

@item Refresh
Refreshes the active view where refresh is supported.

@item Communities
Opens the community browser.

@item Admin
Appears only for administrator sessions.

@item Login/Logout
Starts login or clears the current session.
@end table

@section Navigation Model

Most views are loaded lazily.  Opening a view starts an asynchronous request,
then populates a @code{GtkListBox} when the response is parsed.  Lists that
support pagination use the scrolled window edge-reached signal to request older
items.

@node Accounts and Authentication
@chapter Accounts and Authentication

@section Login

Use @samp{Login} in the header bar or the account section of Settings.
Successful login stores the bearer token, username, and administrator flag in
the session file.  On the next launch, Tweeta Desktop loads the session and
attempts to restore the authenticated state.

If the server reports that the logged-in user is an administrator, the admin
button appears in the header bar and the native admin view becomes available.

@section Logout

Logging out clears the active token, resets authenticated UI state, and removes
the local session file.  It does not invalidate every possible server-side
token unless the server endpoint performs that action.

@section Passkeys

The account settings view includes passkey management.  When the program was
built with @code{USE_FIDO2=1} and libfido2 is available, Tweeta Desktop can use
a local FIDO2 security key for passkey registration and authentication.  When
native support is unavailable, the UI may direct passkey management to the web
settings flow.

@section Passwords and Account Changes

Settings includes account actions for changing username, changing password,
private account state, transparency location, community tag, profile outline
colors, account deletion, follow requests, affiliate requests, and bulk post
deletion.  These actions require login and depend on server permissions.

@section Delegate Accounts

The Settings view includes delegate management:

@itemize @bullet
@item Invite another username as a delegate.
@item View your delegates and accounts you delegate.
@item Accept, decline, or review invitations.
@item Switch back to the primary account.
@item Add or validate linked accounts.
@end itemize

@node Timelines and Posts
@chapter Timelines and Posts

@section Public and Following Timelines

The default view shows public posts.  Use the @samp{Public}/@samp{Following}
toggle to switch timeline type.  Following timeline requests require login.

Infinite scrolling loads older posts when the scrolled window reaches the
bottom.  The client tracks the last visible post ID and asks the server for
items before that cursor.

@section Composing Posts

To compose a post:

@enumerate
@item Log in.
@item Click @samp{Compose}.
@item Enter text in the composer.
@item Optionally attach media or configure a poll when those controls are
available.
@item Submit the post.
@end enumerate

Media uploads are sent to the upload endpoint first.  The returned URL and file
metadata are then included in the post payload.

@section Interacting With Posts

Post widgets support the operations exposed by the server and current user
permissions:

@itemize @bullet
@item Like and unlike.
@item Retweet or undo retweet.
@item Quote.
@item Reply.
@item Bookmark or remove bookmark.
@item React with standard or custom emojis.
@item View reactions, likes, retweets, quotes, or detail pages where supported.
@item Vote in polls.
@item Report a post.
@item Delete or edit your own post where supported.
@end itemize

The client keeps an in-memory interaction cache for like, retweet, and
bookmark state so freshly toggled buttons remain coherent when a server
response omits those fields.

@section Polls

Polls are rendered inline when present in server responses.  Active polls allow
voting, and closed polls display final results.  The parser handles vote
counts, percentages, user vote state, active state, expiration, and quiz-style
score fields where supplied.

@section Media

Images are loaded asynchronously and scaled for display.  Video attachments are
represented with a control that opens the media URL externally.  Spoiler flags
and server-provided file metadata are preserved in the parsed attachment data.

@section Notes and Fact Checks

Administrator sessions can add public notes or fact checks to posts from the
post UI or admin surfaces.  Notes may include severity metadata supplied by the
server.

@node Profiles
@chapter Profiles

Profile views show the user's display name, username, avatar, banner, bio,
details, badges, follower count, following count, post count, and tabbed feeds
for posts and replies.

@section Profile Actions

Depending on the viewed profile and current login state, the profile view can
show controls to:

@itemize @bullet
@item Follow or unfollow.
@item Enable or disable post alerts for the profile.
@item Block or unblock.
@item Mute or unmute.
@item Report.
@item Request affiliation.
@item Open shop or donation flows.
@end itemize

@section Editing Your Profile

When viewing your own profile, @samp{Edit Profile} opens fields for display
name, bio, location, website, pronouns, theme, accent color, profile label,
automated label state, and avatar radius where the account is allowed to set
one.

The profile view also exposes avatar/banner removal where available.  Avatar
and banner uploads use the profile media endpoints.

@node Search and Discovery
@chapter Search and Discovery

@section Search

The header search entry searches both users and posts.  Results are shown in a
search view with separate tabs.  Selecting a user opens that user's profile;
selecting a post opens or interacts with that post according to the available
controls.

@section Explore

The Explore view provides a category selector with server-backed discovery
feeds:

@itemize @bullet
@item Trends.
@item Best of week.
@item Most bookmarked.
@item Most discussed.
@item Longest threads.
@item Posts with media.
@item Posts with polls.
@item Trending users.
@item Suggested users.
@item User directory.
@item Top hashtags.
@item Digest.
@item Leaderboard.
@item Site or explore stats.
@end itemize

Use @samp{Refresh} in the view to reload the selected category.

@node Notifications
@chapter Notifications

Notifications show server events such as likes, retweets, replies, follows,
mentions, quotes, and reactions.  Unread notifications are highlighted.  The
view provides an action to mark all notifications as read, and individual
notification rows can be marked read as the server supports it.

Notification preferences are configured in Settings.  Local switches control
whether notifications, sound effects, and direct-message notifications are
enabled in the running client.  Push notification subscription management is
also available from Settings when supported by the server.

@node Direct Messages
@chapter Direct Messages

@section Conversation List

The Messages view contains ordinary Tweetapus conversations and a separate
encrypted messaging tab.  The ordinary conversation list displays conversation
titles, participants, last message previews, timestamps, and unread counts.

@section Conversation Detail

Opening a conversation shows message history and controls for:

@itemize @bullet
@item Sending a text message.
@item Attaching a file.
@item Replying to a message.
@item Editing or deleting messages where available.
@item Adding or removing participants.
@item Renaming group conversations.
@item Managing disappearing messages.
@item Creating, revoking, or joining invite links.
@item Viewing and managing permissions and participant roles.
@item Viewing pinned messages.
@item Leaving a conversation.
@item Requesting payments through the server's payment integration.
@end itemize

Typing state is sent while the message entry changes, and a conversation is
marked read when opened.

@section Attachments and Payments

DM attachments are uploaded before message creation.  The message payload then
includes the uploaded file URL, name, MIME type, and size.  Payment requests
are created through the MPI request endpoint and then displayed as messages
when the server accepts them.

@node Encrypted Messaging
@chapter Encrypted Messaging

Tweeta Desktop includes an experimental encrypted messaging surface backed by
GPGME.  It is separate from ordinary server-side direct messages.

@section Key Setup

Use @samp{Setup Keys} in the Encrypted tab to initialize local key material.
The GPGME layer can generate a keypair, export the public key, import contact
keys, encrypt, decrypt, sign, and verify messages.

@section Contacts

Use @samp{Add Contact} or @samp{Import Key} to add a contact username and
public key fingerprint.  Contacts are kept in the running encrypted messaging
session.  Selecting a contact loads the in-memory encrypted conversation with
that contact.

@section Transports

Two transport modes are exposed:

@table @samp
@item Direct P2P
Attempts direct peer networking using a listener and explicit peer
connections.

@item Relay Mode
Uses Tweetapus transport endpoints for encrypted payload exchange.  The
payload is encrypted before transport.
@end table

Relay mode is the default because it works without inbound connectivity.  The
direct mode requires the selected host and port to be reachable.

@section Limitations

Encrypted conversations and contacts are session-oriented in the current
client.  Verify fingerprints out-of-band before trusting a contact key.  The
server can still observe metadata for relay-mode delivery, such as timing and
sender/recipient identifiers, even though message content is encrypted.

@node Communities
@chapter Communities

Communities are topic-based spaces with their own timelines, members, access
mode, invitations, and moderation operations.

@section Browsing Communities

The Communities view provides:

@itemize @bullet
@item All communities.
@item Communities you belong to.
@item Trending communities.
@item Recommended communities.
@item Search by query.
@item Join by invite.
@item Create community.
@end itemize

Opening a community displays its timeline and details.

@section Community Timeline

The community timeline supports normal post rendering and infinite scrolling.
The header shows the community name and details.  Actions include members,
invite, manage invites, moderation, style, pin post, edit, access mode, and
delete where permissions allow.

@section Community Administration

Community moderators or admins can:

@itemize @bullet
@item View members.
@item Change member roles.
@item Ban or unban members.
@item Review join requests.
@item Change access mode.
@item Update icon, banner, or tag.
@item Pin or unpin posts.
@item Review moderation logs.
@item Create or revoke invites.
@end itemize

@node Lists
@chapter Lists

The Lists view shows owned and followed lists.  You can create a list, refresh
the list index, open list details, and manage list membership where permitted.

The list detail view contains tabs for list tweets, members, and followers.
Controls include follow or unfollow, edit, delete, and add member.

@node Articles
@chapter Articles

The Articles view shows article entries returned by the server and provides a
@samp{New Article} action.  Article support is server-dependent.  Article
records include title and Markdown body fields in the parsed post structures
when the server supplies them.

@node Settings
@chapter Settings

Settings is a grouped view for user preferences, account operations, and
maintenance.

@section Appearance

Appearance settings include:

@itemize @bullet
@item Light theme.
@item Dark theme.
@item System default theme.
@item Compact mode.
@end itemize

Theme changes update GTK's dark-theme preference for the running process.

@section Notifications

Notification settings include master enablement, sound effects, direct-message
notifications, and push notification subscription management.

@section Content Filters

Content filters include muted words and muted conversations.  The view can add
muted words and refresh muted word/conversation state from the server.

@section For You Interests

The client can display and reset server-side For You interests.  These values
influence server recommendation behavior where the server implements that
feature.

@section Scheduled Posts

Settings includes scheduled post management.  You can create a scheduled post
and refresh pending scheduled posts.

@section Shop

The shop section lists your products, supports product creation, and refreshes
the shop state.  Profile views may expose shop and donation actions for other
users.

@section Data and Cache

The data section displays cache size, clears cache files from the XDG cache
directory, and clears local search history.

@node Administration
@chapter Administration

The Admin button appears only when the authenticated session is an admin
session.  Administrative operations call server-side admin endpoints and are
subject to server authorization.

@section Admin Tabs

The native admin view includes:

@table @samp
@item Stats
Site statistics such as users, posts, suspensions, restrictions, and
verification counts.

@item Users
User search and user actions.

@item Posts
Post search and post moderation.

@item Suspensions
Suspension review and refresh.

@item Reports
Report review and action.

@item Logs
Moderation log search.

@item Blocks
Blocking state review.

@item Emojis
Custom emoji administration.

@item Badges
Badge administration.

@item DMs
Direct-message administration and search.

@item Shop
Product and purchase administration.

@item Communities
Community administration.

@item Notifications
Send a server-side notification to a target user.

@item Clone
Clone selected user state into a new account according to selected categories.

@item Tools
Impersonation, restore-admin, post-as-user, and bulk user edit operations.
@end table

@section Impersonation

The Tools tab can impersonate a user while preserving the original admin token,
username, and admin flag in the session data.  Use @samp{Restore Admin} to
return to the preserved admin context.

Because impersonation changes the active token, verify which username is shown
in the header before taking actions.  The client displays status text when an
admin session is preserved.

@section Bulk User Edits

The bulk edit tool accepts comma-separated targets and a JSON payload.  The
payload is sent to the upstream admin API for each selected account.  Validate
payloads carefully before submitting; this is an intentionally powerful
administrator operation.

@node Files and State
@chapter Files and State

@section Source Tree

Important repository paths:

@table @file
@item src/
C source and header files.

@item Makefile
Primary build, test, install, and documentation targets.

@item meson.build
Meson build definition.

@item tweeta-desktop.texi
