@node Developer Guide
@chapter Developer Guide

@section Coding Style

The project uses procedural C with GLib and GTK conventions:

@itemize @bullet
@item C99 source.
@item GLib scalar types such as @code{gchar}, @code{gboolean}, and @code{gint}.
@item @code{snake_case} functions.
@item GTK widget pointers stored in shared globals where the existing design
requires cross-module callbacks.
@item GLib allocation helpers such as @code{g_new0}, @code{g_strdup}, and
@code{g_free}.
@item json-glib builders and parsers instead of ad hoc JSON strings.
@end itemize

@section Adding a Feature

A typical feature touches:

@enumerate
@item Endpoint constants in @file{src/constants.h}.
@item Data structures in @file{src/types.h} if new parsed state is needed.
@item JSON parsing or payload construction in @file{src/json_utils.c} or a
local action helper.
@item Network/action handlers in @file{src/actions.c} or a focused action
module.
@item Widgets in @file{src/views.c} or @file{src/ui_components.c}.
@item Tests in @file{test_main.c}.
@item Documentation in @file{tweeta-desktop.texi}.
@end enumerate

@section Threading Rules

Network operations that can block should not update GTK widgets directly from
worker threads.  Fetch on a worker thread, store parsed results in an
@code{AsyncData} value, and schedule UI updates with @code{g_idle_add}.

Protect shared state with the existing mutexes where applicable.  The code
uses request IDs to discard stale async responses after rapid refreshes.

@section Upstream Reference Tree

The @file{tweetapus/} directory may contain upstream server source for
reference.  Do not modify it from this repository.  Use it only to understand
request and response shapes when updating the desktop client.

@node Codebase Operation
@chapter Codebase Operation

This chapter explains how the program operates as a running system and how a
maintainer should move through the codebase.  It is deliberately more detailed
than the module map: it follows real control flow, explains ownership and
threading conventions, calls out the places where state crosses module
boundaries, and describes the normal way to add or debug features.

The most important rule is that Tweeta Desktop is not organized around a large
framework.  It is a procedural GTK application.  The user interface is built
explicitly, callbacks are connected explicitly, HTTP requests are made by
explicit helper functions, and parsed data is passed into explicit row builders.
This makes the code easy to search, but it also means maintainers should follow
the local pattern of the nearest feature instead of inventing a second
architecture.

@section Startup Sequence

Execution begins in @file{src/main.c}.  The startup sequence is short, but it
establishes almost every process-wide invariant:

@enumerate
@item @code{gtk_init} initializes GTK and consumes standard GTK command-line
arguments.

@item @code{curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_ALL)} initializes libcurl before any
network helper can run.

@item @code{g_mutex_init(&g_globals_mutex)} initializes the mutex used around
authentication and other shared global state.

@item @code{g_interaction_cache} is created as a string-keyed hash table.  It
stores in-memory interaction flags for posts after the user likes, retweets, or
bookmarks them.

@item A @code{GtkCssProvider} installs application-level CSS classes.  Current
classes include unread notification highlighting, dim labels, and note
severity styling.

@item @code{create_window} constructs the window, header bar, every stack view,
and the long-lived widget globals used by callbacks.

@item @code{load_session} reads the XDG session file into global auth state.

@item @code{update_login_ui} applies the loaded auth state to widgets: compose
sensitivity, login/logout labels, username display, and admin button
visibility.

@item @code{gtk_widget_show_all(window)} shows the fully constructed UI.

@item @code{start_loading_tweets(GTK_LIST_BOX(g_main_list_box))} starts the
initial public-feed load.

@item @code{gtk_main} enters the GTK event loop.

@item After the main loop exits, the process tears down libcurl, interaction
cache state, authentication strings, community state, encrypted messaging
session state, P2P networking, GPGME state, and the globals mutex.
@end enumerate

The ordering is important.  Widgets are created before @code{load_session}, but
@code{update_login_ui} runs immediately after session loading, so the visible
window is shown with the correct login state.  If you add a widget whose
visibility or sensitivity depends on authentication, initialize it in the
logged-out state in @file{src/views.c}, then update it from
@code{update_login_ui} or the relevant login/logout path.

Startup also shows a general architectural property: creation and mutation are
separate.  @file{src/views.c} creates widgets.  @file{src/actions.c} usually
mutates them after data or state changes.

@section Process-Wide State

Process-wide state lives mostly in @file{src/globals.c} and is declared in
@file{src/globals.h}.  This includes:

@itemize @bullet
@item Authentication state such as @code{g_auth_token},
@code{g_current_username}, @code{g_is_admin}, and impersonation fields.
@item Navigation state such as the active timeline type, active profile, active
community ID, and current view widgets.
@item Widget pointers such as @code{g_stack}, @code{g_main_list_box},
@code{g_search_entry}, @code{g_admin_button}, @code{g_notifications_list},
@code{g_conversations_list}, @code{g_dm_messages_list}, and settings widgets.
@item Local preference state such as notification toggles and compact mode.
@item The post interaction cache.
@item The encrypted messaging session and encrypted messaging widgets.
@end itemize

The codebase uses globals because GTK callbacks often need to update widgets
that were created far away from the callback.  That is the established pattern,
but it should still be used deliberately.  Prefer a local context structure
when data only belongs to one dialog or one asynchronous request.  Use a global
only when state must outlive the current callback and be reached by unrelated
callbacks.

Authentication globals should be read or copied through mutex-protected helper
patterns when accessed off the main thread.  For example, the network layer
duplicates the current auth token before placing it into an HTTP header.  Do
not keep raw pointers to global auth strings across asynchronous boundaries.

@section Window and View Construction

@file{src/views.c} owns the main UI layout.  @code{create_window} creates a
GTK toplevel window, attaches a header bar, creates header controls, and then
creates a @code{GtkStack}.  Every major screen is added to that stack with a
stable name such as @samp{timeline}, @samp{profile}, @samp{search},
@samp{notifications}, @samp{messages}, @samp{settings}, @samp{admin},
@samp{bookmarks}, @samp{lists}, @samp{list_details}, @samp{explore},
@samp{articles}, @samp{communities}, and @samp{community_tweets}.

The header controls do very little work directly.  They are wired to callbacks
in @file{src/actions.c}.  A button callback usually switches the visible stack
child, shows the back button if the user is leaving the main timeline, and
starts a loader for the selected view.

Most views are built from standard GTK containers:

@itemize @bullet
@item @code{GtkHeaderBar} for top-level navigation.
@item @code{GtkStack} for screen switching.
@item @code{GtkNotebook} for tabbed profile, search, settings, messages, admin,
and list-detail screens.
@item @code{GtkScrolledWindow} plus @code{GtkListBox} for feeds, search
results, notifications, conversations, communities, lists, and admin rows.
@item @code{GtkDialog}, @code{GtkGrid}, @code{GtkEntry}, @code{GtkTextView},
@code{GtkSwitch}, @code{GtkComboBoxText}, and @code{GtkSpinButton} for forms.
@end itemize

Widget pointers that must be reached by callbacks are stored in
@file{src/globals.c}.  This is the dominant architecture of the project:
callbacks in one module can update widgets created in another module by using
the shared global references declared in @file{src/globals.h}.

The important construction rule is: every long-lived view should be fully
constructed once, then repopulated.  Do not destroy and recreate whole stack
children just to refresh data.  Instead, clear the child list box, insert a
loading row if useful, start a loader, and repopulate the list when the async
operation completes.

@section Navigation and Refresh Semantics

Navigation is mostly controlled by switching @code{g_stack}.  The code uses
fixed stack child names rather than dynamically allocated pages.  This means
callbacks can reliably say:

@example
gtk_stack_set_visible_child_name(GTK_STACK(g_stack), "messages");
@end example

The back button is a simple navigation affordance, not a complete history
stack.  When adding a new secondary view, make sure its entry callback shows
the back button and its exit path returns to a sensible parent view.

Refresh is centralized through @code{on_refresh_clicked}.  It checks the
currently visible stack child and dispatches to the correct loader.  When you
add a new view that should support the header refresh button, update this
dispatch function.  If a view has its own refresh button, wire that button to
the same loader or to a small local callback that calls the same loader.

@section Action Callback Pattern

@file{src/actions.c} is the operational center of the application.  It
contains callbacks for login, logout, refresh, search, compose, post
interaction buttons, profile actions, settings forms, admin tools, direct
messages, list operations, community actions, and async loading.

A common callback follows this pattern:

@enumerate
@item Validate local preconditions, such as login state, selected row data, or
required text fields.
@item Build a URL with endpoint constants from @file{src/constants.h}.
@item Build JSON request data with json-glib when the operation sends a body.
@item Call the network helper, either directly for small operations or in a
worker thread for potentially slow loads.
@item Parse the response or extract an error message.
@item Update widgets immediately for simple state changes, or schedule a full
view reload after the server accepts the operation.
@item Release temporary strings, JSON objects, response buffers, and context
structures.
@end enumerate

The code deliberately favors explicit handlers over a generic command
framework.  That keeps each server operation close to the widget that invokes
it, but it also means maintainers should search for the visible button label,
callback name, or endpoint constant when tracing behavior.

Callbacks generally fall into four categories:

@table @asis
@item View-entry callbacks
These switch the stack and start loading data.  Examples include
@code{on_notifications_clicked}, @code{on_messages_clicked},
@code{on_settings_clicked}, and @code{on_admin_clicked}.

@item Mutating action callbacks
These perform one operation and then update or reload part of the UI.  Examples
include like, retweet, bookmark, follow, block, mute, join community, leave
community, delete post, and admin moderation actions.

@item Dialog callbacks
These build a dialog, collect values if the response is accepted, then call a
perform helper.  Profile editing, password changes, passkey management,
community creation, DM permissions, and admin tools use this pattern.

@item Loader callbacks
These create @code{AsyncData}, start a worker thread, and schedule a GTK-thread
completion callback with @code{g_idle_add}.
@end table

The @code{perform_*} naming convention usually means "execute the server or
state operation and return success or parsed data."  The @code{on_*} naming
convention usually means "GTK callback."  The @code{start_loading_*} naming
convention usually means "begin an asynchronous view reload."

@section The Request Lifecycle

Most server-backed operations follow this lifecycle:

@enumerate
@item The user activates a widget.
@item GTK invokes an @code{on_*} callback.
@item The callback reads widget state, object data, or globals.
@item The callback validates that required state exists.
@item A URL is selected from @file{src/constants.h}, sometimes with
@code{g_strdup_printf} for IDs.
@item If needed, a @code{JsonBuilder} creates the request body.
@item The code calls @code{fetch_url}, @code{fetch_url_with_auth_token},
@code{fetch_url_with_file}, or a higher-level wrapper.
@item The response is parsed with json-glib or one of the parsing helpers in
@file{src/json_utils.c}.
@item The callback updates local state, updates the affected widgets, or calls
a loader to rebuild the affected view from server state.
@item Temporary objects are freed.
@end enumerate

Small operations sometimes execute synchronously in the GTK callback.  Larger
operations that populate lists generally run in a GLib thread.  If an operation
may block long enough to make the window feel frozen, use the async loader
pattern.

@section Loading a Feed

Timeline-like views share a repeatable flow.  For example, loading the public
timeline works as follows:

@enumerate
@item A view or refresh callback calls a loader such as
@code{start_loading_timeline}.
@item The loader clears or marks the target list box as loading, creates an
@code{AsyncData} structure, records request context, and starts a GLib thread.
@item The worker thread constructs the URL and calls @code{fetch_url}.
@item @code{fetch_url} performs the libcurl request and returns a memory
buffer.
@item The worker thread calls a parser such as @code{parse_tweets}.
@item The worker thread stores parsed data and success flags in
@code{AsyncData}, then uses @code{g_idle_add} to schedule the UI completion
callback.
@item The completion callback runs on the GTK main thread, clears loading
state, creates row widgets with helpers in @file{src/ui_components.c}, inserts
them into the list box, frees parsed data, and releases the async context.
@end enumerate

Pagination uses the same shape with @code{is_append} set in the async context
and @code{before_id} set to the last visible item.  Scrolled windows emit
@code{edge-reached}; the handler checks which feed type is attached to the list
box and requests the next page.

Several list boxes carry a @code{feed_type} object-data value.  This lets the
shared scroll handler tell whether it is dealing with public timeline,
following timeline, bookmarks, profile posts, community posts, or another
paginated feed.  When adding pagination to a new feed, set a stable
@code{feed_type} on the list box and extend the edge-reached dispatch.

Feed rows should not assume the server returns every field.  The parsers
already normalize several alternate field names, but UI code should still be
defensive around optional strings, optional counts, optional attachments, and
optional nested quote tweets.

@section Async Data Lifetimes

@code{struct AsyncData} in @file{src/types.h} is the generic carrier for many
background requests.  It can hold a target list box, parsed tweets, users,
notifications, conversations, messages, communities, lists, a profile, a
conversation, request IDs, query strings, pagination cursors, and success
flags.

The normal ownership model is:

@enumerate
@item The starter allocates @code{AsyncData} with zeroed memory.
@item The starter stores copied strings and borrowed widget pointers.
@item The worker thread fills in response-derived data.
@item The worker thread schedules exactly one completion callback with
@code{g_idle_add}.
@item The completion callback owns final cleanup of parsed lists, copied
strings, and the @code{AsyncData} object.
@end enumerate

Do not free @code{AsyncData} in the worker thread after scheduling an idle
callback.  Do not update GTK widgets in the worker thread.  Do not pass stack
allocated objects through @code{g_idle_add}.

Some loaders use request IDs to prevent stale results from overwriting newer
results.  The pattern is: increment or record a current request ID when
starting the request, copy that ID into @code{AsyncData}, and have the
completion callback compare the copied ID against the current global or widget
request ID.  If the request is stale, free its data and skip UI mutation.

@section Main-Thread Rules

GTK is not thread-safe.  All widget creation, insertion, label changes, button
state changes, and stack navigation must happen on the main thread.  Worker
threads can allocate plain GLib data, call libcurl, parse JSON into detached
structures, and prepare strings.  They must hand results back with
@code{g_idle_add} or an equivalent main-context mechanism.

If you see a crash that only happens during fast refreshes or while navigating
away from a view, inspect the async completion callback first.  Common causes
are stale callbacks touching widgets that no longer represent the same request,
missing request-ID checks, or data freed before the idle callback consumes it.

@section Network Request Mechanics

@file{src/network.c} wraps libcurl.  It resolves the API base URL at runtime,
allowing @env{TWEETA_API_BASE_URL} to override compiled constants.  It also
resolves the public base domain through @env{TWEETA_BASE_DOMAIN}; this is used
when the client needs absolute web or media URLs.

Each request allocates a @code{MemoryStruct} response buffer.  libcurl writes
response bytes through @code{WriteMemoryCallback}.  The request layer sets the
user agent, the Tweetapus client header, content-type headers when needed, and
the bearer token header when an authenticated session exists.

When the server requires a CAP challenge token, the network layer can fetch and
solve a challenge through @file{src/challenge.c}, then retry the original
request with the challenge result.  This keeps challenge handling out of most
UI callbacks.

There are two important URL concepts:

@table @code
@item API_BASE_URL
The compiled default API root used by endpoint macros.

@item BASE_DOMAIN
The compiled default web/media origin used when constructing absolute web or
media URLs.
@end table

The runtime rewrite helper checks whether a URL starts with the compiled API
or base domain.  If the user set @env{TWEETA_API_BASE_URL} or
@env{TWEETA_BASE_DOMAIN}, the prefix is rewritten before libcurl sees the URL.
This lets the rest of the code keep using constants from @file{src/constants.h}
without manually threading instance configuration through every call.

The core request helper initializes a response buffer even for empty responses.
If libcurl fails, it frees that buffer and returns @code{FALSE}.  If libcurl
succeeds but the server returns an application-level error, the request helper
usually still returns @code{TRUE}; the caller must parse the response and
decide whether the operation succeeded.

File uploads use the multipart helper path rather than the JSON helper path.
If you are adding a file upload, prefer the existing upload helpers instead of
manually configuring curl MIME state in a feature callback.

@section JSON Parsing and Payload Construction

@file{src/json_utils.c} is the boundary between server JSON and typed C data.
It parses posts, profiles, users, notifications, conversations, messages,
communities, lists, admin objects, login responses, upload responses, and
detail payloads into structures declared in @file{src/types.h}.

Parsers are intentionally tolerant of several server response shapes.  For
example, post lists may arrive under keys such as @code{posts},
@code{timeline}, @code{bookmarks}, or @code{tweets}.  Interaction flags may
also use alternate names.  This tolerance lets the desktop client survive
small server response changes.

Outbound JSON is built with @code{JsonBuilder} and serialized with
@code{JsonGenerator}.  Avoid formatting JSON by hand when adding new payloads;
json-glib keeps string escaping, null handling, booleans, arrays, and nested
objects correct.

Every parser that allocates nested structures has a matching free function.
The UI completion path should use those free helpers after rows have copied or
consumed the needed values.

Parser functions should avoid assuming a field exists unless the server
contract absolutely requires it.  The existing style is:

@itemize @bullet
@item Check @code{json_object_has_member}.
@item Check for JSON null before reading nullable fields.
@item Use @code{g_strdup} for strings stored in structs.
@item Initialize booleans and counts to safe defaults.
@item Parse arrays by iterating elements and appending allocated structures to
a @code{GList}.
@item Free partially constructed objects on parser failure.
@end itemize

When adding a field to a parsed structure, update three places together:

@enumerate
@item The structure in @file{src/types.h}.
@item The parser in @file{src/json_utils.c}.
@item The matching free function in @file{src/json_utils.c}.
@end enumerate

If the field is rendered, update @file{src/ui_components.c} or the relevant
view code.  If the field is important behavior, add or update a parser test in
@file{test_main.c}.

Payload construction has the inverse rule: use @code{JsonBuilder}, include only
fields that should be sent, and match the server's expected names.  For PATCH
style operations, omitting a field and sending it as JSON null may have
different meanings; follow the server endpoint semantics.

@section Widget Row Construction

@file{src/ui_components.c} converts parsed data into concrete GTK rows.  It
builds tweet rows, user rows, notification rows, conversation rows, message
rows, community rows, list rows, shop rows, and other repeated UI elements.

Rows often attach identifiers or state pointers with @code{g_object_set_data}
or @code{g_object_set_data_full}.  Later callbacks retrieve those values to
know which tweet, user, community, list, conversation, or message the clicked
button belongs to.  When attaching allocated strings, use the @code{_full}
variant so GTK releases them with the widget.

Media and avatars are loaded asynchronously by UI utility helpers.  The row can
show a placeholder immediately while a background fetch obtains and scales the
image, then schedules a GTK-thread update.

Rows are where many per-object callbacks are connected.  A tweet row may
contain buttons for reply, like, retweet, quote, bookmark, reactions, notes,
details, poll voting, report, edit, or delete.  Each button needs enough
attached object data to let its callback identify the target post and update
the right visible state.

When wiring row-level callbacks:

@itemize @bullet
@item Attach immutable identifiers such as tweet ID, user ID, username,
conversation ID, community ID, list ID, or message ID to the row or button.
@item Use @code{g_object_set_data_full} for heap strings.
@item Keep mutable UI counters and booleans in a context object only when the
callback needs to update the existing row without a full refresh.
@item If the server response can disagree with an optimistic update, schedule a
reload or update the interaction cache.
@item Avoid storing pointers into parsed objects that will be freed after the
list is populated.
@end itemize

This last point is critical.  Parsed objects are usually freed after widgets
are created.  Widget callbacks must own their own copies of identifiers and
other values they will need later.

@section Session and Authentication State

@file{src/session.c} owns the local session file.  @code{save_session} writes a
JSON object containing the token, username, admin flag, and optional preserved
admin impersonation state.  @code{load_session} restores those values into
globals during startup.  @code{clear_session} removes the file on logout.

Authentication state controls widget sensitivity and visibility.  The compose
button starts disabled and becomes available after login.  The admin button is
hidden unless the session is admin.  Authenticated-only views show an error
dialog or refuse to load when no token is present.

The login path starts in @code{on_login_clicked}.  The dialog collects
credentials, @code{perform_login} posts them to the basic-login endpoint, and
@code{parse_login_response} extracts the token, username, and admin status.
Successful login stores globals, calls @code{save_session}, and updates the UI.

Logout goes through @code{perform_logout}.  It clears in-memory auth state,
clears the local session file, resets impersonation state, updates UI, and
returns the app to an unauthenticated posture.

Native passkey support is conditional.  Code should check
@code{webauthn_fido2_is_enabled} before showing or executing native FIDO2 flows.
When native support is absent, the UI may fall back to a web settings path.

@section Interaction Cache

The interaction cache exists because server timeline responses may not always
echo the latest local interaction state immediately or may use different field
names across endpoints.  @code{update_interaction_cache} stores like,
retweet, and bookmark booleans by tweet ID.  Parser code consults
@code{get_cached_liked}, @code{get_cached_retweeted}, and
@code{get_cached_bookmarked} when response fields are missing.

Use this cache only for short-lived UI coherence.  It is not persistent and is
not a source of truth.  Server reloads and explicit response fields should
still win when they are available.

@section Admin and Impersonation Flow

The admin view is a large native GTK surface over server-side admin endpoints.
It uses the same request, parse, and row-construction model as ordinary user
views, but the callbacks check for admin context before making privileged
requests.

Impersonation changes the active token and username to the target user while
saving the original admin token and username in dedicated globals and the
session file.  Restore-admin reverses that state.  This lets the client survive
restart during an impersonation session, but it also means code that performs
privileged actions must check the preserved admin context where appropriate,
not only the currently displayed username.

Admin actions are intentionally explicit.  Many of them use wrappers such as
@code{perform_admin_request} and admin-specific loaders like
@code{start_loading_admin_users} and @code{start_loading_admin_posts}.  The
admin view is tab-heavy, so each tab owns its own list widget, search entry,
and refresh path.

When adding an admin operation:

@enumerate
@item Add or reuse the endpoint macro in @file{src/constants.h}.
@item Add the widget controls in the appropriate admin tab in
@file{src/views.c}.
@item Connect controls to a clearly named callback in @file{src/actions.c}.
@item Check admin context before making the request.
@item Parse the response enough to distinguish success from server error.
@item Refresh the affected admin list or status label.
@item Add documentation in the Admin chapter and in this operational chapter if
the operation introduces a new pattern.
@end enumerate

@section Direct Message Flow

Ordinary direct messages use server conversations.  The conversation list
loads summaries.  Opening a conversation stores the conversation ID and parsed
conversation detail on the messages list widget.  Sending a message reads the
entry text and any pending attachment context, uploads files first if needed,
builds a message payload, posts it to the conversation endpoint, clears the
composer context, and reloads the message list.

Conversation management actions such as rename, add participant, permissions,
roles, disappearing messages, invite links, pinned messages, leave, edit, and
delete follow the same pattern: read the active conversation ID from widget
data, build the request, and refresh the affected conversation view.

DM state is stored partly in globals and partly as object data on
@code{g_dm_messages_list}.  The list widget can hold the active conversation
ID, parsed conversation detail, pending reply ID, pending file path, pending
file type, a composer status label, and P2P recipient context.  This makes the
message send callback context-sensitive: the same entry and send button can
send either an ordinary Tweetapus DM or a P2P encrypted message depending on
the data attached to the list.

When adding a DM feature, first identify whether it belongs to:

@itemize @bullet
@item The conversation list.
@item The active conversation header.
@item Message row actions.
@item The composer context.
@item Admin DM tooling.
@end itemize

Each location has different state ownership.  Message row actions should keep
message-specific IDs on the row or button.  Conversation-wide actions should
read the active conversation ID from @code{g_dm_messages_list}.  Composer
actions should update and clear composer object data consistently.

@section Encrypted Messaging Flow

Encrypted messaging adds two layers.  @file{src/p2p_crypto.c} manages GPGME
keys and cryptographic operations.  @file{src/p2p_network.c} manages either
direct peer transport or relay transport through Tweetapus endpoints.

The encrypted tab creates a local P2P session after key setup.  Contacts are
stored in a hash table keyed by username, and in-memory conversations are
stored in another hash table.  Sending an encrypted message looks up the
contact fingerprint, encrypts plaintext, records a local message, then sends
the encrypted payload through the selected transport.  Relay mode periodically
polls for incoming encrypted payloads, decrypts them, and inserts them into
the in-memory conversation.

There are two overlapping encrypted messaging implementations in the codebase:
UI/session helpers in @file{src/actions.c}, and transport-specific handlers in
@file{src/actions_p2p_network.c}.  The latter handles transport selection,
direct listener start/stop, peer connection, and contact creation with
transport metadata.  The network layer handles message serialization,
deserialization, relay encoding, direct socket delivery, relay polling, and
incoming message processing.

The important operational boundaries are:

@itemize @bullet
@item @file{src/p2p_crypto.c} should know about keys and cryptographic
transforms, not GTK widgets.
@item @file{src/p2p_network.c} should know about transports and wire payloads,
not ordinary Tweetapus UI rows.
@item The action modules bridge GTK events to crypto/network operations.
@item The encrypted messaging session is in-memory process state.  Do not
assume contacts and conversations survive restart unless persistence is added.
@end itemize

Because encrypted messaging touches threads, GPGME state, network sockets, and
GTK widgets, changes here should be tested manually with debug output enabled
and should keep cleanup paths symmetrical.

@section Settings Flow

Settings is not a passive preference screen.  Many sections are active server
surfaces: muted words, muted conversations, For You interests, scheduled
posts, shop products, delegates, account requests, passkeys, password change,
username change, privacy state, profile outlines, moderation history, block
causes, bulk post deletion, and account deletion.  Each setting either mutates
local process state, sends a server request, or both.

Local-only toggles such as theme and compact mode update process globals and
GTK settings.  Server-backed settings build JSON payloads and call profile,
auth, delegate, or settings endpoints.

Settings is also one of the densest views in the project.  It is built as a
single scrollable vertical surface with framed sections.  Each section tends to
own several globals because refresh callbacks need to repopulate many lists at
once.  The helper @code{on_filters_refresh_clicked} refreshes several settings
subsystems together: muted words, muted conversations, For You interests,
scheduled posts, shop products, delegates, and account requests.

When adding settings UI, avoid creating a new toplevel view unless the workflow
is large enough to justify it.  Most account and preference operations belong
inside the existing Settings stack child.

@section Communities, Lists, Explore, and Articles

These feature areas follow the same architecture as timelines but with
feature-specific view state.

Communities use a browser view and a detail/timeline view.  A community row
stores the community ID.  Clicking the row switches to the community timeline,
stores the active community ID, loads community details, and loads community
posts.  Community management actions read the active community ID from widget
data on the community timeline list.

Lists use an index view for owned and followed lists, then a list-detail view
with tabs for tweets, members, and followers.  List actions should update the
detail view and then refresh the index when membership or ownership state
changes.

Explore is category-driven.  The combo box selects a server discovery endpoint,
and refresh reloads whichever category is active.  Adding a category means
updating the combo box labels, the category dispatch, parser handling if the
shape is new, and documentation.

Articles are exposed as a stack view with a list and a new-article action.
Article records reuse some post structures because the server can return
article title and body fields alongside post-like data.

@section Search Flow

Search begins at the header search entry.  Activating the entry calls the
search callback, which reads the query and runs @code{perform_search}.
Search results are shown in a stack child with tabs for users and posts.

User search results should open profiles.  Post search results should reuse
tweet row rendering where possible so interaction behavior remains consistent
with timeline rows.  When adding new searchable object types, prefer a new tab
or a clearly separated section instead of mixing unrelated row types in one
list without labels.

@section Profile Flow

Profiles are loaded by username through @code{show_profile}.  The profile view
has long-lived widgets for banner, avatar, name, badges, username, bio, status,
details, stats, action buttons, and tabbed feeds.

Profile data has two layers:

@itemize @bullet
@item The profile summary object controls header fields and action-button
visibility.
@item Profile post/reply/media/highlight feeds populate list boxes in tabs.
@end itemize

When changing profile behavior, keep those layers separate.  A failed feed load
should not destroy the profile header.  A profile header reload should not
silently reuse old action-button object data.

Profile editing is dialog-based.  The dialog copies current profile fields
into entries and combo boxes, then submits a PATCH-style update.  Avatar and
banner operations use upload/delete endpoints and then reload the profile.

@section Memory Ownership

The project mixes GTK ownership, GLib containers, and manual C allocation.
Important ownership rules are:

@itemize @bullet
@item Strings returned by GLib helpers are usually freed with @code{g_free}.
@item Parsed lists are freed with @code{g_list_free_full} and the relevant
structure-specific free function.
@item json-glib parser, builder, generator, and root node objects must be
unreferenced or freed after use.
@item Response buffers allocated by the curl write callback must be freed after
parsing.
@item Widget-attached allocated data should use @code{g_object_set_data_full}
when the widget can own the value.
@item GTK updates must happen on the main thread.
@end itemize

The safest way to extend the code is to copy the ownership pattern of the
nearest existing operation with the same shape.

A more concrete ownership checklist:

@itemize @bullet
@item If a function returns a newly allocated @code{gchar *}, the caller frees
it unless the function explicitly documents different ownership.
@item If a parser appends structs to a @code{GList}, the completion path should
use the correct @code{free_*} function for each element.
@item If a widget callback needs an ID after parser data is freed, duplicate
the ID and attach it with @code{g_object_set_data_full}.
@item If a dialog allocates a context struct, connect it to the response signal
and free it in the response handler after reading the widgets.
@item If a libcurl response buffer is passed to a parser, free the buffer after
the parser has copied the fields it needs.
@item If a @code{JsonNode *} is returned by @code{json_builder_get_root}, free
or unref it according to the exact json-glib ownership used in the surrounding
code.
@item If a thread allocates result data for an idle callback, the idle callback
owns cleanup.
@end itemize

Leaks in this application commonly come from early returns in dialog code,
server-error branches that skip response-buffer cleanup, or row callbacks that
attach heap data without a destroy notifier.

@section Error Handling

Most user-visible failures appear as GTK message dialogs.  Server errors are
often extracted from an @code{error} JSON field.  Loader failures usually
replace the target list with a short failure label, while simple button actions
either show a dialog or leave the current view unchanged.

For developer diagnostics, GLib warnings and debug messages are used in lower
layers.  Run with @env{G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all} while diagnosing request and parse
behavior.

Error handling should be local and visible.  If an operation fails because the
user is not logged in, show a direct login-required message.  If the server
returns a structured error, display that text as secondary dialog text when it
is safe to show.  If a background list load fails, put an explanatory row in
the list rather than leaving an old loading row forever.

Do not treat HTTP transport success as application success.  Many helpers
return @code{TRUE} when libcurl successfully received a JSON error response.
The feature callback must inspect the JSON response when the endpoint returns
structured success fields.

@section Adding a Server-Backed View

To add a new top-level or secondary server-backed view, follow this sequence:

@enumerate
@item Add endpoint constants to @file{src/constants.h}.
@item Add data structures to @file{src/types.h} if existing structs do not fit.
@item Add parser and free functions to @file{src/json_utils.c}.
@item Add tests for representative successful, empty, null-field, and error
payloads in @file{test_main.c}.
@item Add widget globals to @file{src/globals.h} and @file{src/globals.c} only
if callbacks need long-lived access.
@item Add a @code{create_*_view} function or extend an existing view in
@file{src/views.c}.
@item Add the stack child in @code{create_window} if it is a new screen.
@item Add entry, refresh, and back-navigation callbacks in
@file{src/actions.c}.
@item Add a @code{start_loading_*} async loader if the view loads remote data.
@item Add row builders to @file{src/ui_components.c} if the view displays
repeated objects.
@item Update @code{on_refresh_clicked} if the header refresh button should
reload the view.
@item Update this manual.
@end enumerate

If the feature is a single button or dialog inside an existing view, skip the
new stack child and add only the callback, request helper, parser/payload code,
tests, and documentation.

@section Debugging Workflow

When debugging a behavior problem, start by classifying the failure:

@table @asis
@item Nothing happens when clicking a control
Search for the button label in @file{src/views.c} or
@file{src/ui_components.c}.  Confirm it has a @code{g_signal_connect}.  Then
inspect the callback's precondition checks.

@item A request is wrong
Search for the endpoint macro in @file{src/constants.h}, then search for that
macro's use in @file{src/actions.c}.  Inspect URL formatting and JSON builder
fields.  Run with @env{G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all} to see network debug output.

@item A response is fetched but nothing renders
Inspect the parser in @file{src/json_utils.c}.  Check whether the server shape
matches the keys the parser accepts.  Add a parser test before changing UI
code.

@item A row renders but its button affects the wrong object
Inspect @code{g_object_set_data} calls in the row builder.  The callback is
probably reading stale, borrowed, or missing object data.

@item A view shows stale data after fast refresh or navigation
Inspect the async request ID and idle callback cleanup path.

@item A crash happens after closing a dialog or navigating away
Inspect ownership of context structs, row-attached data, and idle callback
data.  Use GLib fatal criticals during development to stop at the first
invalid GTK use.
@end table

Useful local commands include:

@example
G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all ./tweeta-desktop
G_DEBUG=fatal-criticals G_MESSAGES_DEBUG=all ./tweeta-desktop
make test
make docs
@end example

@section Practical Search Strategy

Because the code is explicit, source search is usually the fastest way to
orient:

@itemize @bullet
@item Search a visible button label to find where it is created.
@item Search a callback name to find both signal connection and implementation.
@item Search an endpoint macro to find all server operations using it.
@item Search a struct field to find parser, renderer, and free-function
updates.
@item Search a stack child name to find navigation and refresh behavior.
@end itemize

For example, to trace bookmarks, search for @code{BOOKMARKS_LIST_URL},
@code{perform_bookmark}, @code{start_loading_bookmarks}, and the bookmark
button construction in tweet row code.  That reveals the endpoint constants,
mutation helper, view loader, and row-level UI state.

@node Architecture
@chapter Architecture

@section Module Map

@table @file
@item src/main.c
Application startup, CSS initialization, global cache setup, session load,
window creation, main loop, and cleanup.

@item src/views.c and src/views.h
Construction of the main window and primary GTK views.

@item src/actions.c and src/actions.h
Most event handlers, API operations, async loaders, settings handlers,
admin actions, post interactions, profile actions, and utility workflows.

@item src/actions_p2p_network.c
Additional encrypted messaging transport handlers.

@item src/ui_components.c and src/ui_components.h
Reusable row and widget construction for posts, users, notifications,
conversations, communities, lists, and related UI elements.

@item src/network.c and src/network.h
libcurl setup, request dispatch, response buffering, base URL overrides,
headers, and challenge retry behavior.

@item src/json_utils.c and src/json_utils.h
Parsing for posts, profiles, users, notifications, conversations, messages,
communities, lists, admin data, login responses, and payload construction.

@item src/session.c and src/session.h
Session file path, save, load, and clear operations.

@item src/p2p_crypto.c and src/p2p_crypto.h
GPGME key generation, export, import, encryption, decryption, signing, and
verification helpers.

@item src/p2p_network.c and src/p2p_network.h
Encrypted message transport, direct listener, peer connections, relay polling,
wire serialization, and encrypted message dispatch.

@item src/webauthn_fido2.c and src/webauthn_fido2.h
Optional native passkey support using libfido2.

@item src/challenge.c and src/challenge.h
CAP challenge solving helpers used by the networking layer.

@item src/globals.c and src/globals.h
Shared application state and widget references.

@item src/types.h
Shared data structures.

@item src/constants.h
Default URLs and endpoint format strings.
@end table

@section UI Layer

GTK widgets are arranged around a @code{GtkStack}.  The header controls switch
visible stack children and start relevant loaders.  Lists are generally
@code{GtkListBox} widgets inside @code{GtkScrolledWindow} containers.

@section Network and Parsing Layer

The network layer returns response buffers.  JSON parsing converts buffers into
typed C structures.  UI code receives lists of parsed structures and constructs
rows.  Free functions in @file{json_utils.c} release nested data.

@section State Layer

Global state tracks authentication, current username, admin state, active
profile, active community, current timeline type, notification preferences,
interaction cache, and widget references.  This keeps callback wiring simple,
though contributors should avoid adding new globals when a local context
structure is sufficient.

@node Testing
@chapter Testing

Run tests with:

@example
make test
@end example

The test runner uses GLib's testing framework and links against the modular
application components.

Current test areas include:

@itemize @bullet
@item Tweet parsing, including attachments, media, notes, and polls.
@item Login response parsing and admin flag handling.
@item JSON payload construction for posts and DMs.
@item Session save, load, clear, and XDG path override behavior.
@item Profile and profile replies parsing.
@item Search user parsing.
@item Notification parsing.
@item Conversation and message parsing.
@item Tweet detail parsing.
@item Challenge solver behavior.
@item Poll parsing and memory cleanup.
@item Community parsing and memory cleanup.
@item Upload response parsing.
@item Timeline type validation.
@item Optional integration login flow controlled by environment variables.
@end itemize

When adding server-facing behavior, prefer parser and payload tests that cover
missing fields, null fields, alternate server key names, and failure payloads.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@section Build Cannot Find GTK, curl, json-glib, or GPGME

Install the development packages and verify pkg-config:

@example
pkg-config --cflags gtk+-3.0 json-glib-1.0 gpgme
pkg-config --libs gtk+-3.0 json-glib-1.0 libcurl gpgme
@end example

@section PDF Generation Fails

Install GNU Texinfo and a TeX distribution that provides @command{texi2pdf}.
Then rerun:

@example
make pdf
@end example

@section Login Does Not Restore

Check the session file under @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tweeta-desktop/session.json}
or @file{~/.config/tweeta-desktop/session.json}.  Logging out removes this
file.  If the server invalidated the token, log in again.

@section Wrong Server Instance

Confirm both server overrides are set.  The API URL should usually include
@file{/api}; the base domain should usually not.

@example
TWEETA_API_BASE_URL="https://host.example/api"
TWEETA_BASE_DOMAIN="https://host.example"
@end example

@section UI Does Not Update After an Action

Use the view's refresh button or navigate away and back.  Some server
operations are eventually consistent or return a minimal response that requires
a reload before all counts and flags are visible.

@section Passkey Button Opens Browser Flow

Native passkey support requires a build with @code{USE_FIDO2=1}, libfido2 at
build and runtime, a supported security key, and compatible system device
permissions.

@section Encrypted Messages Do Not Arrive

For direct P2P mode, verify the listener host and port are reachable from the
peer.  For relay mode, verify the active account, contact key fingerprint, and
server relay support.  Also verify the local GPG key can decrypt messages.

@node Security and Privacy
@chapter Security and Privacy

@section Session Token Storage

The session file contains authentication material.  Tweeta Desktop attempts to
write it with user-only permissions.  Protect backups and avoid sharing the
file.  Remove it manually or log out if a token should no longer be used.

@section Administrator Sessions

Admin tools can alter users, posts, communities, notifications, and
impersonation state.  Use a dedicated admin account where possible, verify the
active identity in the header, and treat bulk edit JSON as privileged input.

@section Encrypted Messaging Trust

GPG encryption protects message content from passive transport readers when
keys are correct, but it does not automatically solve key authenticity.
Confirm fingerprints through a trusted channel.  Relay transport can still
expose metadata.

@section Local Data

The cache directory may contain downloaded media or derived cache data.  Use
@samp{Clear Cache} in Settings or remove the XDG cache directory if local
media residue matters.

@node Packaging
@chapter Packaging

Packagers should usually build with:

@example
make PREFIX=/usr
make DESTDIR="$pkgdir" PREFIX=/usr install
@end example

Recommended package dependencies:

@itemize @bullet
@item GTK3 runtime.
@item libcurl runtime.
@item json-glib runtime.
@item GPGME runtime.
@item libfido2 runtime only when built with @code{USE_FIDO2=1}.
@end itemize

Recommended package outputs include:

@itemize @bullet
@item Executable in @file{/usr/bin}.
@item Desktop file in @file{/usr/share/applications}.
@item Icon in @file{/usr/share/pixmaps}.
@item Man page in @file{/usr/share/man/man1}.
@item Info manual in @file{/usr/share/info}.
@item License text from @file{COPYING}.
@end itemize

After installing an Info file, distribution packaging systems often run
@command{install-info} in maintainer scripts or equivalent package hooks.

@node Reference
@chapter Reference

@section Environment Reference

@table @env
@item TWEETA_API_BASE_URL
API endpoint root.  Example: @code{https://example.invalid/api}.

@item TWEETA_BASE_DOMAIN
Web and media domain root.  Example: @code{https://example.invalid}.

@item XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Configuration root used for the session file.

@item XDG_CACHE_HOME
Cache root used by cache management.

@item G_MESSAGES_DEBUG
GLib debug category selector.

@item G_DEBUG
GLib debug behavior selector.
@end table

@section Make Variable Reference

@table @code
@item CC
C compiler.  Defaults to @command{gcc} when unset.

@item PREFIX
Install prefix.  Defaults to @file{/usr/local}.

@item SRCDIR
Source directory used by install paths.  Defaults to @file{.}.

@item USE_FIDO2
Set to @code{1} to compile native libfido2 passkey support.

@item MAKEINFO
Texinfo Info/HTML generator.  Defaults to @command{makeinfo}.

@item TEXI2PDF
Texinfo PDF generator.  Defaults to @command{texi2pdf}.

@item DESTDIR
Packaging staging root used by @command{make install}.
@end table

@section Exit Behavior

The graphical application normally exits when the main window closes.  Build
and documentation commands use the exit status of the compiler or Texinfo tool
that failed.

